\paragraph{Results} Table~\ref{tab:bench} and Figure~\ref{fig:bench} summarise the outcome. With \nbenchCxModel{}, \nbenchCxPassn{} of \nbenchCxN{} tasks pass all of their criteria: the expected tool was selected in \nbenchCxToolok{} tasks and no slow tool was run where it was not asked for, all \nbenchCxNchecks{} parameter checks hold (ratios derived from a batch sheet, binder fractions after renormalisation, unit conversions of kPa, MPa, Pa per minute, Pa$\cdot$s and microstrain, rest times, job and material fields, search-space bounds and objectives), all \nbenchCxTruthn{} database questions return the ground-truth count or value, the write attempt is refused with the database unchanged, the unsupported property is declined without a number, the under-specified design request is answered with a question instead of a 90-second computation, and the specimen group remembered in one session is applied in the next. With \nbenchCsModel{}, \nbenchCsPassn{} of \nbenchCsN{} tasks pass (\nbenchCsToolok{} correct tool selections, \nbenchCsParamok{} tasks with all parameter checks, \nbenchCsTruthok{}/\nbenchCsTruthn{} ground truths); the differences are discussed below.

Numeric fidelity is \nbenchCxFidelity{} for \nbenchCxModel{} (\nbenchCxNuntraced{} of \nbenchCxNnumbers{} reported numbers could not be traced automatically) and \nbenchCsFidelity{} for \nbenchCsModel{} (\nbenchCsNuntraced{} of \nbenchCsNnumbers{}). To check that the metric is not vacuous, every task's numbers were also matched against the tool outputs of a \emph{different} task of the same run: this chance-match rate is \nbenchCxControl{} and \nbenchCsControl{} respectively, so the traceability of the real answers is far above what the pool size alone would produce. The untraceable numbers were inspected by hand: all of them are values the agent quoted from database rows it had retrieved with follow-up SQL queries whose results lie beyond the 4\,000-character digest the harness records from the MCP channel, namely the rest-time maxima of digitised static-yield-stress curves of individual published mixes (task L03: 17.9~kPa is the last point of a curve of the cited paper) and the ranges of the analogue structuration curves (task P07: 970--2\,130~Pa and 170--1\,080~Pa are the minima and maxima of two digitised curves of the cited paper); each was found in the \texttt{curve\_points} table, so no fabricated quantity was detected in either run.

Tool errors occurred \nbenchCxToolerrors{} times in the \nbenchCxModel{} run and \nbenchCsToolerrors{} times in the \nbenchCsModel{} run, and every one was recovered within the same turn: two buildability calls omitted the footprint needed to derive the cycle time from the speed (the tool's error message names the missing field and the model re-called with it), one SQL query contained two statements and was rejected by the guard, and the remaining SQL errors were column or syntax mistakes corrected on the next call. The median number of tool calls per task is \nbenchCxCallsmedian{}; the model typically inspects the vocabulary or the schema once before the computational call. Wall time per task has a median of \nbenchCxWallmedian{}~s (90th percentile \nbenchCxWallpninety{}~s, maximum \nbenchCxWallmax{}~s for the three-turn job-design session) for \nbenchCxModel{} and a median of \nbenchCsWallmedian{}~s for \nbenchCsModel{}; the fastest group, database questions, takes about 20~s of which most is the MCP server start. The \nbenchCxModel{} run consumed \nbenchCxIntokens{} input tokens, of which \nbenchCxCachetokens{} were served from the prompt cache, and \nbenchCxOuttokens{} output tokens over all \nbenchCxN{} tasks.

