183: 
184: \paragraph{Literature retrieval and SQL} Literature retrieval ranks published mixes against the same target list used for design: tier \emph{exact} contains mixes whose measured values satisfy every target; tier \emph{near} contains the closest misses. For arbitrary questions, a schema-inspection tool returns tables, columns and sample rows of either database, and a SQL tool executes a single SELECT statement under two independent guards (a keyword filter that rejects anything but SELECT/WITH, and an SQLite authorizer that denies every write and schema operation), with a 20~s timeout and a 200-row cap; results above 20 rows are returned to the model as a digest and saved in full as CSV.
185: 
186: \subsection{Tool registry and schema projection}
187: 
188: Each tool is registered once with a name, a description, a JSON schema for its arguments, the Python function, and flags (read-only, slow, timeout). The registry is projected to the Anthropic tool format, the OpenAI function format and the MCP \texttt{tools/list} response, so the three are consistent by construction (a test asserts their equality). Table~\ref{tab:tools} lists the \nnTools{} tools. Every tool returns a \texttt{ToolResult} with a JSON data block, a one-line summary and a list of artifacts (tables, images, markdown, xlsx) written to the session's artifact directory; the model sees at most 20 rows of a table and a truncated JSON, the interface renders the artifacts with download buttons. Natural-language dictionaries are normalised by the same rules the former form interface used: binder fractions are renormalised to one with a warning, admixtures are percentages of binder by product mass, curing can be a preset name or a word (\texttt{water}, \texttt{moist}, \texttt{sealed}, \texttt{ambient}, \texttt{steam}), and an unknown material class raises an error that names the closest valid classes so that the model can correct itself.
