197: \subsection{Agent core}
198: 
199: \paragraph{System prompt with live context} The system prompt is rebuilt for every turn from the live state of the package: the role, the data sizes, the model table with training size, grouped-CV quality and weak flags, the material-class vocabulary, specimen groups and curing presets, a mix-dictionary example, the guardrails, the user's durable notes and the rolling summary of the session. Because the model table is generated from the trained artifacts, retraining the models changes what the agent promises without any prompt edit.
200: 
201: \paragraph{Guardrails} Nine rules are stated in the prompt. The ones that matter for engineering use are: (1) never invent numbers --- every quantitative statement must come from a tool result in this conversation, and predictions are written as $q_{50}\,[q_{10}, q_{90}]$ with units; (2) targets flagged weak are presented as order-of-magnitude guidance with published analogues as primary evidence, and extrapolation beyond the training range (reported by the tool as range violations) is stated; (3) defaults filled in for values the user did not give are named; (4) database questions go through schema inspection before SQL, with explicit distinction between paper counts and mix counts; (5) slow tools run immediately only when targets and search space are given, otherwise one clarifying question is asked, once; (6) a non-printable verdict is accompanied by actionable alternatives (cycle-time window, layer height, required $\tau_{s,0}$ or $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$, accelerator, VMA, lower $w/b$); (8) a tool error is explained in one line and the input is corrected and retried at most twice; (9) the models are correlations in published data and do not replace experiments.
202: 
203: \paragraph{Tool loop and memory} A turn runs until the model stops calling tools or twelve iterations are reached. Transcripts, metadata and a rolling summary are stored per session; when a transcript exceeds 40\,000 characters the older part is summarised by a cheap model call and replaced by the summary in the prompt. Notes saved with the \texttt{remember} tool are appended to a durable file that enters the system prompt of every later session, which is how a laboratory's conventions (default specimen, preferred units) persist. Sessions can be resumed from the interface or the command line.
204: 
205: \subsection{LLM backends and interfaces}
206: 
207: The provider interface is a single generator per turn that yields text deltas, tool calls, tool results and a final usage record. Four providers implement it: the Anthropic Messages API and the OpenAI Chat Completions API with native function calling (tools executed in-process); the Claude Agent SDK, which runs the bundled Claude Code CLI under the machine's Claude subscription and serves the registry as an in-process MCP server; and the OpenAI Codex CLI, which runs under a ChatGPT subscription and reaches the tools through a stdio MCP server started as a child process. The provider is chosen in the order API key, Claude login, ChatGPT login, or set explicitly. Two details of the Codex path were necessary in practice: recent versions of the CLI refuse MCP tool calls in non-interactive mode unless the server pre-approves its tools, and the CLI blocks on an open standard-input pipe, so it is started with a closed stdin. A fifth, scripted provider supports tests without an LLM. The web application (Streamlit) is a chat with inline tool status, rendered tables and figures, download buttons, backend and session selection and example prompts; a terminal REPL and a one-shot mode share the same engine; the MCP server can be attached to Claude Code, Codex or Cursor directly. Prompts, labels and the system prompt are available in Korean and English. For distribution to students the package is zipped with the trained models, both databases, launcher scripts and a setup guide; the only external requirement is Python and, for the ChatGPT path, Node.js for the Codex CLI.
