216: \paragraph{From a batch sheet to a prediction} Figure~\ref{fig:caseP05} shows the most common laboratory input, a per-cubic-metre batch sheet. The agent derives the ratios the model needs ($w/b = 227/650 = 0.349$, $s/b = 2.0$, binder fractions 0.77/0.23, PCE 0.5\,\% of binder), calls \texttt{predict\_properties} once for both targets, and reports each prediction with its 80\,\% interval and the defaults it assumed (28~d, 50~mm cube, moist curing). The width of the shrinkage interval, more than a factor of two, is a property of the literature pool that the model is instructed to pass on rather than hide.
