292: \end{figure}
293: 
294: \paragraph{What the benchmark caught} The first execution of the benchmark on \nbenchCxModel{} passed 29 of 33 tasks, and three of the four failures were not language-model errors but integration defects that the case studies had not revealed: the Codex CLI rejects the sandbox option on a resumed thread, so the second turn of every multi-turn session failed (tasks P08 and J02); the MCP server is started by the CLI with a clean environment, so the isolated home directory of the harness did not reach the \texttt{remember} tool and the note was written elsewhere (task M01); and the unsupported-property task was scored as a failure only because the agent's phrasing (``do not support'') was not in the keyword list. The two provider defects were fixed, the keyword list was extended, the three tasks were rerun from fresh sessions and the final numbers above are reported; the first-run figures are kept here because finding such defects cheaply, before a printer is involved, is what a machine-checkable task set is for. The \nbenchCsModel{} run, executed after the provider fix, passed 31 of 33 on first scoring; both misses were again wording rather than behaviour: for the unsupported property the model wrote that chloride migration is ``absent from the available prediction targets'' and, instead of stopping there, queried the database for a measured analogue and reported it explicitly as an analogue and not a prediction, and for the unknown material it chose the valid class \texttt{volcanic\_ash} and said the tuff was ``represented by'' it. Both phrasings were added to the accepted wording lists, which are part of the released task file. The two models differ mainly in style and cost: \nbenchCsModel{} inspects the schema and vocabulary more often (median \nbenchCsCallsmedian{} calls per task against \nbenchCxCallsmedian{}), quotes more numbers (\nbenchCsNnumbers{} against \nbenchCxNnumbers{}), and takes longer (median \nbenchCsWallmedian{}~s against \nbenchCxWallmedian{}~s); its untraceable numbers (\nbenchCsNuntraced{}) are, like those of \nbenchCxModel{}, values from retrieved rows beyond the recorded digest (the static yield stress and structuration rate of a similar published print, the water-to-binder ratios of two retrieved mixes, and one sand-to-binder ratio the model computed from the retrieved component masses), each found in or derived from the databases.
295: 
296: % =====================================================================================================================
297: \section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}
298: 
299: \paragraph{What the agent adds} The computational layer existed before the agent, with a form interface and a command line. What changed with the agent is who can ask and what can be asked in one breath: a batch sheet becomes a prediction, a print job becomes a verdict, a window and a design specification, and a question about the literature becomes a SQL query, without the user learning the vocabulary of 138 features or the YAML of a design specification. The second change is the explanation: because the tool results are structured, the agent can say which defaults it used, how wide the interval is and why, and which published mixes support the answer. The dam-monitoring system of Liu et al.\ \cite{liu2026llm4shm} and the structural-analysis assistant of Avila et al.\ \cite{avila2025hai} reach similar conclusions for their domains; our contribution is to show that the pattern holds when the tools are statistical models with wide intervals and physics with calibrated uncertainty, and to measure the agent layer with a benchmark rather than with cases alone.
300: 
301: \paragraph{Faithfulness is a property of the architecture, not of the model} The guardrails ask the model not to invent numbers, but what makes the request enforceable is that every number has a traceable origin in a tool result, which is what the numeric-fidelity metric checks. The residual untraceable numbers in the benchmark were, on inspection, values quoted from tool-written tables beyond the recorded digest, not fabricated measurements; the metric reports them anyway, which is the behaviour one wants from an audit, and the chance-match control shows that the metric would have exposed a model that quotes numbers from the wrong place.
302: 
303: \paragraph{Limitations} (i) The rheology models are wide: the 80\,\% intervals of static yield stress, $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$ and flow exceed a decade, so the feasibility probability of design candidates for printing targets is low (around 0.3) and the actionable output is often the schedule window rather than a single mix. The main cause is that published static yield stresses mix rest protocols and devices; protocol-normalised targets are the next step in the data layer. (ii) The buildability physics is linear structuration with a free-wall buckling bound; prints longer than about 90~min and braced or closed paths need the exponential structuration law and section-specific buckling factors, which the user can partly compensate with the filament and buckling switches. (iii) The benchmark checks tool use, parameters, ground truth and faithfulness, not the engineering adequacy of the free text; a user study is the natural complement. (iv) Subscription backends are suitable for personal local use only; a shared deployment needs API keys, and the Codex path pays a model-loading cost per turn that an in-process backend does not. (v) Tool descriptions were written in Korean for the originating laboratory and are translated by the model when the interface runs in English; the benchmark was run in English, so this is not a hidden advantage, but native English descriptions would remove a source of variance. (vi) As for every system of this kind, the LLM can misread an unusual phrasing; the parameter checks of the benchmark are the mechanism we propose for catching such errors before they reach a printer, and the released harness makes it cheap to add a failing phrasing as a new task.
304: 
305: \paragraph{Generalisation} The registry pattern transfers to other computational layers in construction materials (durability models, LCA calculators, standards checkers) by registering functions; the backends, the interfaces and the benchmark harness are independent of the tool set. Because the model table and the vocabulary enter the prompt from the live package, the agent follows the data layer as it grows.
306: 
307: % =====================================================================================================================
308: \section{Conclusions}\label{sec:conclusion}
309: 
310: We presented an LLM agent that gives natural-language access to a validated computational layer for 3D concrete printing: \nnModels{} literature-trained property models, an inverse mix-design engine, a physics-based buildability calculator calibrated on \nprintRuns{} published print runs, literature retrieval and guarded SQL over a database of \npapers{} papers. The \nnTools{} tools are defined once and projected to the Anthropic, OpenAI and MCP schemas, so the agent runs on API keys or on Claude and ChatGPT subscriptions, in a chat application, a terminal or any MCP client. Case studies demonstrated end-to-end print planning in conversation, and a 33-task benchmark with machine-checkable expectations measured tool selection, parameter extraction, ground-truth answers, refusals, write resistance and numeric fidelity. The code, the trained models, both databases, the benchmark tasks and the harness are released with the package. Future work will add protocol-normalised rheology targets to narrow the intervals that currently limit design for printing, extend the buildability physics to exponential structuration and reinforced sections, and evaluate the agent with practitioners.
