\section{Methodology}\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Overview}

Figure~ef{fig:arch} shows the five layers of the system. Users reach the agent through a chat web application, a terminal, or an external MCP client. The agent core builds a system prompt with live context, runs the tool loop, and keeps session memory. All tools are defined once in a registry and projected to the schema each LLM backend expects. The computational layer is the existing prediction and design package: property models, inverse design, buildability physics, literature retrieval and guarded SQL. The data layer holds the literature database and the print-process database. The LLM never computes a property; it decides which tool to call with which arguments, and it explains the results.

 egin{figure}[p]
\centering
esizebox{	extwidth}{!}{\input{figures/architecture}}
\caption{System architecture. The LLM (agent core) plans and explains; every quantitative result comes from the computational layer through a tool whose schema is defined once and projected to the Anthropic, OpenAI and MCP formats.}
\label{fig:arch}
\end{figure}

Figure~ef{fig:seq} shows how one print-planning conversation maps onto tool calls: the LLM parses the job and the material, calls the buildability tool, explains the verdict, turns the job into a design specification on request, and saves a report.

 egin{figure}[t]
\centering
esizebox{	extwidth}{!}{\input{figures/sequence}}
\caption{A print-planning conversation as a sequence of tool calls. Solid arrows are structured requests, dashed arrows are results; the notes summarise what each tool computes.}
\label{fig:seq}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Data layer}

\paragraph{Literature database} The literature database was built in our previous work with an LLM-assisted extraction pipeline in which every paper is turned into a structured record (papers, materials with chemistry and physical properties, mixes and components, tests and protocols, measurements with reported and canonical units, digitised figure curves, unmapped terms and quality exceptions) and every record must pass a frozen machine validator before it enters the database; a sample of papers is re-verified against the source by independent agents and the error rate is reported. The pipeline follows the structured-extraction approach of \cite{dagdelen2024structured,polak2024extracting}. At the time of writing the database holds 
papers{} papers (
papersIncluded{} included after full-text screening, 
papersThreeDCP{} of them 3DCP studies), 
mixes{} mixes, 
measurements{} measurement rows of which 
measurementsValued{} carry a tabulated value, and 
curves{} digitised curves with 
curvePoints{} points. Units are harmonised per quantity after merging, and the basis of every ratio (per gram of binder, of paste, of cement) is recorded explicitly.

\paragraph{Print-process database} For the buildability layer, 
printPapers{} 3DCP papers were re-read for printing-process variables and outcomes: printer and nozzle, layer height and width, print speed, layer cycle time, number of layers, height reached, start time after mixing, open time, outcome and failure mode, and the authors' own verdict as a printability label with its basis and confidence. The result is 
printRuns{} print runs (
printPrintable{} printable, 
printBorder{} borderline, 
printNon{} non-printable), 
printCollapses{} collapses, 
printSTF{} stack-to-failure tests, and 
protocolRows{} rheology-protocol rows that record how each static yield stress was measured (device, rest time). Runs are keyed to the mixes of the literature database so that fresh properties and print outcomes can be joined.

\subsection{Computational layer}

\paragraph{Property models} A mix is represented by 
nFeatures{} features: binder composition by material class, water-to-binder and sand-to-binder ratios, admixture, fibre and nano dosages, oxide chemistry and fineness of the materials (class medians when a paper does not report them), and test conditions (age, specimen comparability group, curing, rest time, 3DCP flag). 
nMixesNorm{} of the 
mixes{} mixes could be normalised to this representation. For each of 
nTargets{} target quantities a bundle is trained: a LightGBM point model \cite{ke2017lightgbm}, $q_{10}$ and $q_{90}$ quantile models whose width is rescaled by split-conformal calibration \cite{romano2019cqr}, and a paper-bootstrap ensemble for an epistemic spread. A second, 3DCP-specific variant is trained for targets with enough 3DCP rows and is selected automatically when a query is flagged as 3DCP and the variant's grouped-CV quality is higher, giving 
nModels{} models in total. All quality metrics are computed with GroupKFold by paper, so no mix of a test paper is seen in training \cite{kapoor2023leakage}; Figure~ef{fig:models} shows the rank correlation and training size per target. Models flagged \emph{weak} (few papers, poor interval coverage or low rank correlation; most rheology and flow targets) are reported to the LLM in the system prompt, which is instructed to present them as order-of-magnitude guidance and to lean on published analogues.

 egin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=	extwidth]{fig_models.pdf}
\caption{Paper-grouped cross-validation quality (Spearman rank correlation between predicted and measured values, left) and training size (right) of the general and 3DCP-specific property models. Low-ranked targets are flagged weak in the system prompt.}
\label{fig:models}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{Inverse design} A design specification lists targets (at least, at most, range, goal; with units, conditions and a minimum probability of satisfaction), a search space (system type, $w/b$ and $s/b$ ranges, allowed binder classes with bounds and required flags, admixture ranges, curing) and objectives (clinker fraction, embodied CO$_2$, cost, or per-MPa variants). The engine draws a Sobol sample of the space, predicts every target with its interval, screens candidates by the product of per-target satisfaction probabilities, performs a non-dominated sort on objectives and feasibility, selects a diverse shortlist and refines it by differential evolution over the forward models. Every candidate is returned with its applicability-domain distance and with the closest published mixes as analogues. Curve-derived targets (shear stress at a given shear rate from the Bingham flow curve; static yield stress after a given rest time from the structuration curve) are available as targets, which is what links inverse design to buildability.

\paragraph{Buildability and schedule} A print job is described by object type (wall, hollow cylinder, column, other), target height $H$, footprint (wall length or cylinder diameter), number of filaments across the wall thickness, nozzle, layer height $h$ and width, print speed, layer cycle time $t_c$, start time after mixing, open time and a load safety factor $s_f$. Unstated fields are filled from the medians of printable published runs ($h = 0.5\,d_{\mathrm{nozzle}}$, $w = 1.2\,d_{\mathrm{nozzle}}$, 40~mm/s, $t_c$ = path length / speed) and the agent is required to say so. The fresh material is given either as measured values ($	au_{s,0}$, $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$, density, $E/	au_s$) or as a mix, in which case the property models supply them. With linear structuration $	au_s(t) = 	au_{s,0} + A_{\mathrm{thix}}\, t$ \cite{perrot2016structural,roussel2018rheological}, the bottom layer of an $n$-layer stack built at one layer per $t_c$ yields when $ho g n h > \sqrt{3}\,	au_s(n t_c)$, so that
 egin{equation}
n_{\max}^{\mathrm{plastic}} =  rac{\sqrt{3}\,	au_{s,0}}{ho g h / s_f - \sqrt{3} A_{\mathrm{thix}} t_c},
\qquad 	ext{unlimited if } \sqrt{3} A_{\mathrm{thix}} t_c \ge ho g h / s_f .
\label{eq:plastic}
\end{equation}
For a free straight wall clamped at its base the critical height follows Suiker's self-weight buckling solution with the green modulus $E(t) = (E/	au)\,	au_s(t)$ \cite{suiker2018mechanical}:
 egin{equation}
l_{\mathrm{cr}} = \left(  rac{7.8373\, E(t)\, t_w^2}{12\, ho g} ight)^{1/3},
\label{eq:buckling}
\end{equation}
with $t_w$ the wall thickness (filaments $	imes$ layer width); closed sections are reported as not buckling-critical. The open-time check requires start time plus print duration to stay within the open time. The verdict (printable, borderline, non-printable) names the governing mode and reports $n_{\max}$ with and without the safety factor; the \emph{schedule} sweeps $t_c$ and print speed and returns the window between the minimum cycle time required for stability and the maximum allowed by the open time, or, when the window is closed, the $	au_{s,0}$ and $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$ that would open it.

The criteria are calibrated on the print-process database. For the 
rFailN{} stack-to-failure collapses (
rFailPapers{} papers) whose mix has a tabulated static yield stress, the ratio $R_{\mathrm{fail}} = ho g H_{\mathrm{collapse}} / (\sqrt{3}\,	au_s)$ has median 
rFailMedian{} with interquartile range 
rFailPtf{}--
rFailPsf{} (Figure~ef{fig:rfail}); $R = 1$ is the static criterion with $	au_{s,0}$ alone, and the excess is the structuration accrued during the print. The empirical distribution function of $R_{\mathrm{fail}}$ turns the static ratio of a job into an empirical collapse probability that is reported next to the physical verdict. The tabulated static yield stress alone separates collapsed from successful runs with an area under the ROC curve of only 0.59 over 611 runs, because published values mix rest protocols and because structuration, not $	au_{s,0}$, carries tall stacks; this is why the physics route with structuration is the primary verdict. Extrudability windows (10th--90th percentiles of printable runs: $	au_s$ 273--4920~Pa, plastic viscosity 2.7--57~Pa$\cdot$s, flow table spread 126--197~mm) are used as caution flags, not hard limits. Finally, 	exttt{design\_for\_print\_job} converts a job into a design specification by adding the derived target ``static yield stress after a rest time equal to the print duration $\ge s_f\,ho g H / \sqrt{3}$'', an $A_{\mathrm{thix}}$-maximising steering objective and the extrudability window of the nozzle class, runs the inverse design, and re-assesses every candidate against the job.

