\paragraph{What the benchmark caught} The first execution of the benchmark on \nbenchCxModel{} passed 29 of 33 tasks, and three of the four failures were not language-model errors but integration defects that the case studies had not revealed: the Codex CLI rejects the sandbox option on a resumed thread, so the second turn of every multi-turn session failed (tasks P08 and J02); the MCP server is started by the CLI with a clean environment, so the isolated home directory of the harness did not reach the \texttt{remember} tool and the note was written elsewhere (task M01); and the unsupported-property task was scored as a failure only because the agent's phrasing (``do not support'') was not in the keyword list. The two provider defects were fixed, the keyword list was extended, the three tasks were rerun from fresh sessions and the final numbers above are reported; the first-run figures are kept here because finding such defects cheaply, before a printer is involved, is what a machine-checkable task set is for. The \nbenchCsModel{} run, executed after the provider fix, passed 31 of 33 on first scoring; both misses were again wording rather than behaviour: for the unsupported property the model wrote that chloride migration is ``absent from the available prediction targets'' and, instead of stopping there, queried the database for a measured analogue and reported it explicitly as an analogue and not a prediction, and for the unknown material it chose the valid class \texttt{volcanic\_ash} and said the tuff was ``represented by'' it. Both phrasings were added to the accepted wording lists, which are part of the released task file. The two models differ mainly in style and cost: \nbenchCsModel{} inspects the schema and vocabulary more often (median \nbenchCsCallsmedian{} calls per task against \nbenchCxCallsmedian{}), quotes more numbers (\nbenchCsNnumbers{} against \nbenchCxNnumbers{}), and takes longer (median \nbenchCsWallmedian{}~s against \nbenchCxWallmedian{}~s); its untraceable numbers (\nbenchCsNuntraced{}) are, like those of \nbenchCxModel{}, values from retrieved rows beyond the recorded digest (the static yield stress and structuration rate of a similar published print, the water-to-binder ratios of two retrieved mixes, and one sand-to-binder ratio the model computed from the retrieved component masses), each found in or derived from the databases.

